Help me build ScreenForge as a working, testable MVP. Do not merely produce a plan or a large code dump. Inspect the existing repository first, explain what exists, then implement one milestone at a time.

PRODUCT DECISIONS ALREADY CONFIRMED
- Initial target: Google Chrome on Windows.
- MVP capture: screen video + microphone audio only.
- Webcam recording is excluded from the MVP.
- Recording target: up to 30 minutes.
- Recording controls: start, pause, resume, stop.
- Processing architecture: HYBRID (Option C).
- Browser: capture, UI, transcript display, cut review, responsive preview skipping, and local downloads where feasible.
- External AI provider: transcription and optional cut planning.
- Backend: secure provider access, processing orchestration, and FFmpeg final rendering if required.
- Authentication: Google sign-in is desired, but do not build it before the core capture-to-export path works unless I explicitly request it.
- AI editing: user can choose review-before-apply or automatic application.
- Cut restoration is required; full edit history is not required.
- If AI processing fails after the retry policy is exhausted, preserve the recording and offer manual editing.
- Local-first storage and direct export are preferred.
- Do not claim all data stays local if any audio/video is sent to a backend or AI provider.

UNRESOLVED — DO NOT SILENTLY DECIDE
- Website versus Chrome extension distribution.
- Final export formats and quality controls.
- Persistent project reopening after the browser closes.
- Crash recovery.
- Exact retry counts and retry timing.
- Temporary media retention period.
- Authentication details beyond the desire for Google sign-in.
- Maximum resolution, frame rate, and file-size limits.

FIRST ACTION
1. Inspect the repository and report the framework, folder structure, package manager, existing features, tests, and known problems.
2. Identify the smallest working path from recording to downloadable original file.
3. Propose a short milestone plan.
4. Do not rewrite the whole repository.
5. Do not add unrelated features.
6. Do not expose secrets or ask me to paste API keys into source code.

IMPLEMENTATION RULES
- Work on exactly one milestone at a time.
- Before editing, list the files you intend to create or modify and why.
- Reuse the existing project conventions unless they are clearly broken.
- Use TypeScript types for frontend data and typed request/response models on the backend where applicable.
- Keep UI components, business logic, provider adapters, validation, and media processing separated.
- Keep provider keys in server-side environment variables.
- Add `.env.example` with placeholder names only. Never put real secrets in it.
- Add or update `.gitignore` so secrets, generated media, temporary uploads, build outputs, and local environment files are not committed.
- Validate all API inputs and AI outputs.
- Never trust model-generated JSON, timestamps, filenames, paths, or cut ranges without validation.
- Avoid constructing shell commands by concatenating user-controlled strings.
- Add useful errors and logs without logging API keys, private transcript content, or unnecessary personal data.
- Handle loading, empty, success, and failure states in the UI.
- Keep the original recording unchanged while editing a cut schedule.
- Make cut restoration reliable.
- Ensure preview skips cannot loop forever on overlapping or adjacent cut ranges.
- Preserve audio/video synchronization in the final output.
- Do not fake functionality with buttons that do nothing.
- Do not claim a feature is complete until you have run its tests.
